{
  "feedback": "The candidate's main weakness is its speed. The rationale says the finish needs about 1.25 m/s, but the contract gives a 3.0 s limit and a course roughly 4 to 5 m long, so finishing needs an average of about 1.33 to 1.67 m/s. At base_speed 1.2, the robot would likely cover only about 3.6 m in 3 s, so completion (+500) is almost certainly out of reach even if it stays on the line. Development telemetry (entity R2B, iteration 0) shows the baseline at base_speed 1.6 covered only 0.10 to 0.15 of the course before going off track, so the limiting failure is line tracking, not speed. Slowing down does not address that, and it gives up the completion bonus. The geometry changes are reasonable: iteration 0 logged 1 to 2 line-loss events per run with only five 8 mm sensors spanning ±2 cm, so widening to seven sensors over ±3 cm with 12 mm footprints targets the coverage problem directly. The kp increase from 3 to 5 with kd raised to 0.3 is a sensible response to steady drift, and the weights in [-2, 2] stay within the contract. Revise the speed back up and keep the rest, so the next race tests tracking at a speed that can finish. Do not state outcomes for this candidate in the next revision's rationale.",
  "requested_changes": [
    "Set controller.base_speed back to 1.6 (or within 1.5 to 1.7). The contract's 3 s limit on a 4 to 5 m course needs about 1.35 to 1.65 m/s, and iteration 0 showed the baseline failing at 1.6 from line loss, not from speed, so 1.2 cannot complete.",
    "Keep the seven-sensor geometry (x from -0.03 to +0.03 at 0.01 m spacing, y=0.05, sensor_size 0.012) and sensor_weights [-2, -1.33, -0.67, 0, 0.67, 1.33, 2]. Iteration 0 logged 1 to 2 line-loss events per run with five 8 mm sensors covering only ±2 cm, so this coverage change is the right test of that cause.",
    "Keep kp 5.0 and kd 0.3, but if the revised race shows steering_oscillation above about 0.2 with max_error still near 0.06, lower kp to 4.0 before adding more damping. Iteration 0 showed steering oscillation of 0.08 to 0.15 and max_error about 0.06 m, so the drift is the main problem, not jitter."
  ],
  "decision": "revise",
  "_presentation": {
    "entityR1": {
      "label": "Revise: restore speed to 1.6",
      "notes": "The candidate's base_speed of 1.2 cannot cover the 4 to 5 m course in 3 s, and the baseline's failure was line loss rather than speed. The seven-sensor geometry and kp/kd changes are kept for the next race. The feedback rests on the iteration 0 development telemetry and the contract's time limit."
    }
  }
}
